  "Number": 3,
  "Question": "If \\(x=\\dfrac{1-i\\sqrt3}{2},\\) where \\(i=\\sqrt{-1},\\) then \\(\\dfrac{1}{x^2-x}\\) is equal to:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(-2\\)",
    "B": "\\(-1\\)",
    "C": "\\(1+i\\sqrt3\\)",
    "D": "\\(1\\)",
    "E": "\\(2\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "Compute \\(x^2-x\\) before taking its reciprocal, using \\(i^2=-1\\). Squaring gives\n\\[\nx^2=\\frac{(1-i\\sqrt3)^2}{4}\n=\\frac{1-2i\\sqrt3-3}{4}\n=\\frac{-1-i\\sqrt3}{2}.\n\\]\nThe imaginary parts cancel when we subtract \\(x\\):\n\\[\nx^2-x\n=\\frac{-1-i\\sqrt3-(1-i\\sqrt3)}2\n=-1.\n\\]\nThis denominator is nonzero, so \\(\\dfrac1{x^2-x}=-1\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{B}\\).",
